WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:03.743
Hi! I'm Sandra, from HEARa.

2
00:00:03.743 --> 00:00:05.786
Welcome to Day 1

3
00:00:05.787 --> 00:00:07.640
of the Lip Reading Challenge,

4
00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:10.054
designed to help you practice

5
00:00:10.054 --> 00:00:11.950
and develop your lip 
reading skills!

6
00:00:12.779 --> 00:00:16.235
Hearing loss can have a profound impact on your life.

7
00:00:16.311 --> 00:00:17.336
It touches every interaction.

8
00:00:17.769 --> 00:00:19.515
It can affect your career,

9
00:00:19.552 --> 00:00:20.951
your relationships,

10
00:00:20.951 --> 00:00:22.316
and your happiness.

11
00:00:22.654 --> 00:00:27.740
Join me to explore tips and 
tricks on this YouTube channel

12
00:00:27.866 --> 00:00:31.321
to bring confidence and clarity to relationships

13
00:00:31.322 --> 00:00:32.831
in all areas of your life.

14
00:00:34.372 --> 00:00:38.614
Today you're going to get three mangoes with one stone,

15
00:00:38.614 --> 00:00:40.899
and by that I mean

16
00:00:40.899 --> 00:00:43.859
there's a way to be 
efficient when you're learning how to read lips.

17
00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:48.973
You can put three mangoes in your bucket with

18
00:00:48.973 --> 00:00:52.128
the sounds P, B, and M.

19
00:00:53.658 --> 00:00:59.717
To make these sounds, your lips meet and then part.

20
00:00:59.717 --> 00:01:03.480
There's almost like a puff of air when the mouth opens.

21
00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:05.665
Watch these words:

22
00:01:05.665 --> 00:01:06.635
Park

23
00:01:07.906 --> 00:01:09.853
Bark

24
00:01:10.058 --> 00:01:11.353
Mark.

25
00:01:11.541 --> 00:01:14.042
You may have noticed

26
00:01:14.155 --> 00:01:16.334
that these words look exactly the same!

27
00:01:16.334 --> 00:01:19.214
If you saw these words in isolation,

28
00:01:19.214 --> 00:01:22.872
meaning they're not in a sentence,

29
00:01:22.873 --> 00:01:24.396
you would not be able to tell them apart.

30
00:01:25.462 --> 00:01:26.604
But 

31
00:01:26.774 --> 00:01:30.233
these words don't 
sound the same at all.

32
00:01:30.233 --> 00:01:32.454
Would you really confuse them?

33
00:01:33.199 --> 00:01:35.202
Yeah, you might.

34
00:01:35.203 --> 00:01:39.000
The difference between 
a P and a B is

35
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.189
only a fraction of a second

36
00:01:41.189 --> 00:01:42.556
of vibration of your vocal cords.

37
00:01:43.233 --> 00:01:46.820
If your attention 
is elsewhere for that fraction of a second,

38
00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:53.391
or if there's noise in the background covering 
up a word or two, you might confuse them.

39
00:01:53.530 --> 00:02:01.132
I do find it helpful to know that there's a good 
reason to misunderstand when I get off track.

40
00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:07.560
So you know that you can't distinguish 
between Park, Bark and Mark.  

41
00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:14.580
But if you saw them in a sentence, you'd be able 
to figure it out. You can develop this skill to  

42
00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:20.040
the point where you can do it in real time. So 
if the first thing you see doesn't make sense,  

43
00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:24.900
you can do a quick substitution in your 
mind and come up with something that fits!  

44
00:02:25.403 --> 00:02:28.712
I'll give you an example from my own life.

45
00:02:28.712 --> 00:02:33.792
I'm deaf, and I have a cochlear implant in one ear

46
00:02:33.792 --> 00:02:35.906
and a hearing aid in the other.

47
00:02:35.906 --> 00:02:38.430
I wasn't wearing either (of them) when I saw,

48
00:02:39.094 --> 00:02:40.045
"You should coping."

49
00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.775
You should coping?? That doesn't make sense!

50
00:02:45.775 --> 00:02:49.680
What was actually said is, "You should go big."

51
00:02:50.196 --> 00:02:58.698
Both COPING and Go BIG start and end with 
invisible sounds.

52
00:02:58.698 --> 00:03:01.476
The C in COPING is invisible,

53
00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.648
as is the G in Go Big.

54
00:03:05.649 --> 00:03:09.615
At the end of these words, the -NG found in COPING

55
00:03:09.615 --> 00:03:13.512
and the G in BIG are also invisible.

56
00:03:14.001 --> 00:03:17.287
And everything else looks the same.

57
00:03:17.287 --> 00:03:24.462
So I ended up getting it by making that quick substitution and also because of a gesture.

58
00:03:24.462 --> 00:03:26.290
He said, "You should go big."

59
00:03:27.004 --> 00:03:35.104
I want you to learn to make substitutions 
in today's lesson. Let's try it. I'm going  

60
00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:41.220
to say a sentence with a P, B, or M error 
and I want you to make the substitution.  

61
00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:48.540
I'll give you an example. If you saw, "Have 
you ever been to Banff National Mark?"  

62
00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:54.000
Then you would think, okay, so Banff National Mark

63
00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:59.829
isn't right, how about Banff National Bark? No, that's not it.

64
00:03:59.829 --> 00:04:02.592
Okay, Banff National Park.

65
00:04:02.705 --> 00:04:05.554
You see how that goes.

66
00:04:05.554 --> 00:04:10.136
For the next 10 sentences, I'm not going to give you the answers

67
00:04:10.136 --> 00:04:17.021
but the answers are written below in the video caption section just below the video frame.

68
00:04:17.021 --> 00:04:21.528
If you need more time, just pause the video and repeat.

69
00:04:21.529 --> 00:04:24.429
I'm going to jump in every once in a while

70
00:04:24.429 --> 00:04:27.539
and give you some information on lip reading theory.

71
00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.616
Are you ready?

72
00:04:30.936 --> 00:04:37.139
Number 1: I have a great recipe for barbecue rims.

73
00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:43.380
Number 2: My cub is empty.

74
00:04:46.053 --> 00:04:51.544
Number 3: Ugh,  I'm at the end of my Rome!

75
00:04:52.251 --> 00:04:57.300
Number 4: I don't let my met up on the couch.

76
00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:04.600
Number 5: Please, think no poor about it!

77
00:05:04.601 --> 00:05:12.240
Number 6: Oh, it's so nice to beat you, finally!

78
00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:19.860
In this example, there's a mismatch between 
my facial expression and body language and  

79
00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:22.126
the word "beat."

80
00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:29.038
This mismatch is a clue. 
Was it sarcasm? Or did you misunderstand?

81
00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:34.920
So in this way, lip reading becomes about also 
looking at the facial expression and the body  

82
00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:38.235
language and any gestures involved.

83
00:05:38.235 --> 00:05:41.305
For this reason, the more correct term is speech reading.

84
00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:47.520
I don't think it matters which term you 
use: lip reading (or) speech reading.  

85
00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:52.860
As long as you make use of all the 
information that's available to you--  

86
00:05:53.520 --> 00:06:02.039
so, facial expression, body language, 
gestures, and also the teeth, the tongue,  

87
00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:05.234
the lips, and the jaw.

88
00:06:05.234 --> 00:06:07.271
Let's keep going.

89
00:06:07.736 --> 00:06:13.008
Number 7: The baby sat on his mother's lamb.

90
00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:21.840
Number 8: In yoga class, we chanted home.  

91
00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:27.722
The H is invisible. Watch: home.

92
00:06:28.775 --> 00:06:36.815
Number 9: My hair is a mess-- I need a hope.

93
00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:41.104
The H is invisible, remember?

94
00:06:41.104 --> 00:06:43.923
What is another invisible sound that you learned about in this lesson

95
00:06:43.923 --> 00:06:51.806
that you could put at the beginning of HOPE and then make a P, B, or M substitution at the end?

96
00:06:53.836 --> 00:06:58.980
I'll say it again: My hair is a mess-- I need a hope.

97
00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:03.784
Number 10:  Same thing as number 9.

98
00:07:03.785 --> 00:07:09.125
Make a substitution of the invisible sound at the beginning

99
00:07:09.125 --> 00:07:13.740
and then make a P, B, or M substitution at the end.

100
00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:20.580
Kelp! I'm being chased by a wild pore!

101
00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:32.100
If this exercise did not make sense to you, read 
the answers in the description section below the  

102
00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:40.080
video frame and then repeat the lesson. There's 
also some extra substitution practice for any  

103
00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:43.847
keeners out there that are alongside the answers.

104
00:07:43.847 --> 00:07:47.344
I love keeners (sign for same).

105
00:07:47.985 --> 00:07:53.794
One more tip. P, B and M are homophenes.

106
00:07:53.795 --> 00:08:01.401
Homophenes are groups of sounds that look alike. And when we're learning how to lip read,

107
00:08:01.401 --> 00:08:06.464
we learn about the sounds in groups 
of homophenes, where possible.

108
00:08:06.464 --> 00:08:08.988
It's efficient, but it also calls attention

109
00:08:09.139 --> 00:08:13.993
to the fact that sometimes 
it's understandable to misunderstand.

110
00:08:13.993 --> 00:08:16.805
And then we can deal with it by making that quick substitution

111
00:08:16.805 --> 00:08:22.287
when the first thing we see does not make sense!

112
00:08:22.287 --> 00:08:26.084
Remember the invisible sounds that we 
learned about today.

113
00:08:26.536 --> 00:08:28.285
Make a list:

114
00:08:28.285 --> 00:08:29.520
The H,

115
00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:32.919
the hard C as in coping, or the K sound;

116
00:08:35.551 --> 00:08:36.778
the G,

117
00:08:36.778 --> 00:08:40.227
and the -NG at the end of coping.

118
00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:46.200
There are others, so just keep a running 
list and we'll add to it in future lessons.  

119
00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:53.399
Tomorrow, we're going to practice actually 
lip reading P, B and M sounds in sentences.  

120
00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:59.433
Until then, what word looks like mango?

121
00:08:59.433 --> 00:09:05.798
The word I'm thinking of is not exactly the same, but it looks pretty close.

122
00:09:05.798 --> 00:09:10.104
I really look forward to hearing how you did on these exercises.

123
00:09:10.104 --> 00:09:14.880
Please be sure to comment and 
share any questions that you might have.

124
00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:20.940
Be sure to like the video 
and subscribe to my channel,  

125
00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:25.221
so that you can follow the next nine 
days of our lip reading challenge!  

126
00:09:25.221 --> 00:09:27.671
So, I'll see you tomorrow!

127
00:09:28.310 --> 00:09:29.449
It's time to eat.

128
00:09:29.449 --> 00:09:32.092
I think I'll make a smoothie.

129
00:09:32.186 --> 00:09:33.964
Bye!